WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.740
Android Logic flows from a retrofit source through a repository into a view model and to compose UI.

2
00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:13.300
While React Native often appears disorganized, modern libraries align with this exact same structural grid.

3
00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:17.000
The labels change, but the directional data flow remains the same.

4
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:21.700
Building and React Native does not require adopting an entirely foreign mental model.

5
00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:28.900
Instead, success depends on identifying how the structural principles you already use in Kotlin manifest in this different syntax.

6
00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:31.900
The first rule of this mapping is a strict bifurcation.

7
00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:35.900
You must treat state you own locally differently than state owned by a remote server.

8
00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:44.900
While early React Native development often mixed these concerns into a single store, modern libraries provide dedicated tools that mirror the separation found in Native development.

9
00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:52.900
We are going to map the standard Android stack, view models, repositories, and shared preferences directly to their highly optimized JavaScript counterparts.

10
00:00:52.900 --> 00:01:04.900
By applying native architectural rigor, you ensure that even while navigating the nuances of a new language, the underlying system remains as predictable and scalable as a native code base.

11
00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:14.900
At the simplest level, component level state that isn't shared between screens, like a mutable state and a composable, maps directly to react use state hook.

12
00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:25.900
For more complex state machines, where you might use a sealed class UI state in Android, React Native uses a typescript discriminated union, managed by the use reducer hook.

13
00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:34.900
The challenge arises with global UI state, data that must persist across screens, which is typically handled by a view model and exposed via state flow.

14
00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:39.900
This is where Zewstand fits in, the direct equivalent to the Android view model.

15
00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:49.900
Notice how a state update travels through both pipelines. Zewstand holds the state outside the component tree, letting the UI react without owning mutation logic.

16
00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:56.900
Unlike other JavaScript options, Zewstand requires no boilerplate, no complex reducers or provider wrapping.

17
00:01:56.900 --> 00:02:02.900
It is a plain object store that any component can subscribe to. It uses a selector pattern.

18
00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:09.900
By calling use authstore and selecting only the user object, the component only re-renders when that specific field changes.

19
00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:21.900
This is the functional equivalent of using stateflow.map and collect a state in compose. Both mechanisms exist to prevent unnecessary UI updates from irrelevant state changes.

20
00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:29.900
Zewstand provides the same encapsulation and reactivity you expect from a view model, ensuring the UI remains a pure reflection of the underlying state.

21
00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:38.900
UI state is only half the story. The other half is server state, data owned by an API that exists outside the app's immediate control.

22
00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:45.900
This data has a distinct life cycle. It requires caching, background refetching to stay current, and logic for handling pagination.

23
00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:54.900
In Android, you manage this manually within a repository class, orchestrating co-routines, retrofit interfaces, and room.

24
00:02:54.900 --> 00:03:02.900
TANstack Query consolidates that entire manual infrastructure into a single hook-based system, acting as an automated repository layer.

25
00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:10.900
Read operations, your suspend fun calls, map to the use query hook, which manages the fetch and the cache entry automatically.

26
00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:18.900
Write operations, post-put and delete, map to the use mutation hook, giving you built-in tracking for loading and error states.

27
00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:27.900
For the transport layer itself, you can use the standard Axios library, or the native fetch API. Both serve the same purpose as retrofit or k-tor.

28
00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:35.900
And the asynchronous logic is a direct translation. Kotlin co-routines map cleanly to JavaScript async await and promises.

29
00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:40.900
A common mistake is trying to manage this remote data inside a local store like Zewstand.

30
00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:47.900
Without the specialized logic of TANstack Query, you lose the ability to handle stale data and background synchronization.

31
00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:57.900
TANstack Query ensures that network data is managed as a separate entity, keeping your local UI state machine focused on client-owned interactions.

32
00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:07.900
Finally, we have local persistence for small pieces of data, like off tokens or user preferences. On Android, this is the domain of shared preferences or data store.

33
00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:15.900
Historically, react native relied on async storage, which is often slow and requires asynchronous calls for every read and write.

34
00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:23.900
This chart shows the performance gap. The bottom bar represents async storage. MMKV is the high-speed alternative.

35
00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:29.900
Because it is a synchronous C++ backed store, it can be 10 to 100 times faster.

36
00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:39.900
Just like shared preferences, MMKV is for simple key value pairs. If your app requires relational data, the equivalent of room, you would use Expo SQL instead.

37
00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:49.900
By utilizing MMKV, you eliminate the IO bottlenecks often found in cross-platform apps, bringing storage performance back in line with native expectations.

38
00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:59.900
With each layer defined, we can see how these tools align into a single cohesive stack. This mapping provides a structural mirror for your native skills.

39
00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:05.900
On the left, the Android patterns you know. On the right, the react native libraries that fulfill them.

40
00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:10.900
ZooStan manages the local UI state and view transitions, replacing the V-model.

41
00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:21.900
TanStack Query handles the network lifecycle as an automated repository layer. MMKV provides the fast synchronous persistence you expect from shared preferences.

42
00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:28.900
When you use the specific stack, the resulting application feels structurally identical to a high-quality native built.

43
00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:38.900
While these abstractions are powerful, truly mastering the platform requires understanding the bridge between the JavaScript environment and the native code beneath it.

44
00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:50.900
In the next module, we will explore the new architecture, diving into Hermes, the JSI, and how to write turbo modules in Kotlin. React Native isn't an abandonment of your engineering principles.

45
00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:56.900
It is a parallel environment where your existing architectural rigor is your most valuable asset.

